



needs to be implemented:


If it is not one of your built-in commands, 
check if it is an absolute path (a command starts with '/') 
or a relative path containing a '/' and run that if it is executable (use access(2) to check).

If the command is neither of the above cases then search for the command in 
the search path by looping through the $PATH (i.e., the set of possible executable directories) 
stored as a linked list as given in the skeleton code Download skeleton code. Use access(2) 
in a loop to find the first executable in the $PATH for the command. 
snprintf(3) would be useful to use here (as using strcat() can be problematic).

Once you find the command, execute it using execve(2). 
You also need to have the shell do a waitpid(2) and print out the exit code of the command if it is non-zero (like echo $? in Bash). 
Look into using the WEXITSTATUS macro from <sys/wait.h> Links to an external site..

If the command entered is neither one of your built-in commands nor in the search paths, 
print "command: Command not found." where command is the (wrong) command name that was entered. 

Before executing a command (built-in or external) 
your shell should print out what it is about to execute 
in the form "Executing [pathname]" (for built-ins, print "Executing built-in [built-in command name]").











Just needs some polish / I think they are done:

SIGINT (Ctrl-C),  SIGTSTP (Ctrl-Z), and SIGTERM should be ignored by your shell (but not necessarily by any child process). 
See sigaction(2). Note that when you are running a command inside your shell and press control-C, 
signal SIGINT is sent to both your shell process and the running command process (i.e., all the processes in the foreground process group).



Your shell must support the special * and ? "wild card" 
characters to match file names in arguments for the built-in 
list command and all external commands. It's recommended that you use the existing glob(3) 
library function (it's probably easier to read docs than to write your own working code). 
If there are no matches, the "original" arguments should be passed to execve() or list.